RAWM(1)

# NAME

rawm - Ra window manager for X

# SYNOPSIS

*rawm* [*-C*] [*-c* _path_] [*-v*]

# DESCRIPTION

*rawm* is a dynamic X11 window manager derived from *dwm* 6.0. It owns client
admission, monitor and tag state, layouts, focus, geometry, external-dock work
areas, pointer-driven client manipulation, and the rawm control/state membrane.

Global keyboard policy, program launching, persistent desktop chrome, status
collection, volume control, system-tray ownership, and session lifecycle are
separate authorities. In a rawmkit desktop those responsibilities are normally
provided by *rawkeys*, *rawpanel*, and *rawsession*.

rawm provides tile, floating, monocle, bottom-stack, horizontal bottom-stack,
and grid layouts. Per-tag state includes layout, master count, and master
factor. Windows can carry one or more tags, and each monitor independently owns
its selected view and layout state.

Mapped EWMH dock windows remain external surfaces rather than admitted rawm
clients. Their struts determine usable work areas. Fullscreen clients continue
to use the complete monitor rectangle.

rawm can present a transient OSD for window-manager state transitions such as
layout, view, routing, fullscreen, master-area changes, tag naming, and optional
live floating geometry. The OSD is the only rawm-owned presentation surface; it
is not admitted as a client and does not reserve work area.

# OPTIONS

*-c* _path_, *--config* _path_
	Load the built-in defaults followed by exactly _path_, bypassing the standard
	system and user configuration files.

*-C*, *--check-config*
	Validate the effective configuration and exit without opening the X display.

*-v*, *--version*
	Print the version and exit.

# CONTROL AND STATE INTERFACE

rawm owns the per-screen X selection *\_RAWM\_CONTROL\_SN*, where _N_ is the X
screen number. The selection owner is an unmapped rawm-owned window and is the
liveness boundary for the external control/state protocol. If rawm loses its X
connection, X destroys the owner window and clears the selection.

The owner publishes *\_RAWM\_CONTROL\_VERSION*,
*\_RAWM\_SUPPORTED\_COMMANDS*, *\_RAWM\_STATE*, and *\_RAWM\_CLIENTS*.
Consumers observe these authoritative snapshots instead of reconstructing rawm
semantics from EWMH or the root-window client tree.

Commands are versioned ClientMessage events of type *\_RAWM\_COMMAND* sent to
the owner window. The command surface contains window-management operations
only. Program spawning, panel visibility, status, volume, global-key/XKB policy,
and session lifecycle are intentionally outside the rawm membrane.

The exact version-1 state, client-inventory, command, and argument grammar is
documented in _docs/control-protocol.md_ in the source tree. The standalone
*rawctl* utility is the normal command-line client for this contract.

# EXTERNAL DOCKS AND WORK AREA

rawm treats windows advertising *\_NET\_WM\_WINDOW\_TYPE\_DOCK* as external
desktop surfaces rather than managed clients. A mapped dock that publishes
*\_NET\_WM\_STRUT\_PARTIAL* reserves the corresponding root-screen edge;
legacy *\_NET\_WM\_STRUT* is also accepted.

Reservations are reevaluated when docks map or unmap, when a strut or window
type changes, and when a dock disappears. rawm recomputes monitor work areas
and rearranges tiled clients accordingly. The contract is standard EWMH and
contains no rawpanel-specific lifecycle or configuration dependency.

# COMPOSITOR INTEGRATION

rawm publishes descriptive private properties on admitted clients:

*\_RAWM\_FLOATING*
	CARDINAL 0 or 1 describing the effective floating/elevated presentation
	plane.

*\_RAWM\_SELECTED*
	CARDINAL 0 or 1. One only for the globally selected admitted client.

*\_RAWM\_LAYOUT*
	UTF8_STRING naming the selected layout: *tile*, *floating*, *monocle*,
	*bstack*, *bstack_horizontal*, or *grid*.

*\_RAWM\_MONITOR*
	CARDINAL monitor number, starting at zero.

The transient OSD additionally publishes *\_RAWM\_SURFACE* with value *osd*
and advertises *\_NET\_WM\_WINDOW\_TYPE\_DOCK*. These properties are outputs,
not commands.

# POINTER POLICY

rawm retains pointer-driven client manipulation because it depends directly on
admitted client geometry and active pointer grabs. The stock client bindings
are:

*Meta-Button1*
	Move the focused client while dragging. A tiled client becomes floating after
	movement exceeds the snap threshold.

*Meta-Button2*
	Toggle the focused client between explicit floating and tiled state.

*Meta-Button3*
	Resize the focused client while dragging. A tiled client becomes floating
	after resizing exceeds the snap threshold.

Global keyboard bindings are not rawm authority. Use *rawkeys* or another
external key-binding authority to invoke rawm commands through the control
membrane.

# CONFIGURATION

*rawm* loads window-management policy from *rawm.conf*(5). The normal search
order is built-in defaults, _/etc/rawm/rawm.conf_, then the XDG user file. An
explicit *-c* path replaces the standard system/user file search after built-in
defaults.

During the extraction transition the parser still accepts several deprecated
panel/status/global-key settings so existing layered configurations can be
migrated without a runtime authority rollback. rawm no longer executes those
settings. They will be removed from the schema together with the corresponding
integration-policy migration.

Use *rawm -C* before restarting after an edit.

# SIGNALS

*SIGHUP*
	Restart rawm in place using the original argv.

*SIGTERM*, *SIGINT*
	Cleanly terminate rawm.

# SEE ALSO

*rawm.conf*(5), *rawctl*(1), *rawkeys*(1), *rawpanel*(1), *rawsession*(1)

